A||N||6||87 sc n Louisana||help my 1987 2.5l4 sports coupe has a ecm from a 85 Grand Am??||||5||
Z||000000||87 sc n Louisana||03-09-2000||01:23 AM||||Hey all you people I have a couple of questions?<BR>1 my 87 sc 2.5 has a 1985 grand am computer on it??<BR>2 I know this isnt right!<BR>3 This cant be helpn my car run top notch because the 85 g-am has a dist. my 87 fiero doesnt?<BR>4 My fiero has a weird idle problem cold start up hits 3 grand then goes down to around 1500 till warms up then around 1100 warm it's a five speed and it will also idle up and down a-lot at stop light and sometimes it does,sometime's it dont?<BR>Could this be that the computer is doing this I have changed all the sensor's etc and everything else?<BR>Car has around 96k miles on it help me please <BR>I had noticed befor but not paid much mind to it tell a friend pointed it out to me today that it was marked on with a yellow marker(junkyard of course) and said 85 g-am tech4 2.5l4cly?<BR>any help is great <BR>Thanks guys and girls||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||03-09-2000||09:47 AM||||I don't know enough about the PROM chips to know what the exact differences in programming are.  But I know that in addition to the DIS ignition, the 87 Iron Duke 4-banger has a redesigned intake manifold and higher-flow TBI than the 85 had (resulting in 98 hp vs. 92).<P>Many Fiero engine swappers have lots of good 4-banger ECMs lying around.  Ed Parks quoted me $50 for an ECM for my 88 Coupe - about $150 less than the local NAPA wanted.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||03-09-2000||09:50 AM||||Sorry - I clicked "Submit" before I meant to!  I was going to close by saying I'm not sure if the new ECM will fix your problem, but for $50, I'd want to be sure my Fiero had the proper ECM.<P>I just noticed that in the Mall, a couple of folks offered to help Boilermaker out with a V6 ECM for free!  God bless Fieroholics (snif snif).||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-09-2000||10:35 AM||||I believe the ECM is different for the DIS engines, but I'm not sure what problems it may create if you're using an older ECM. I was also thinking if you have an auto tranny, you need to get an ECM for an auto too, but I'm not 100% sure on that one. Hopefully, TerryK will respond with additional info.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||03-09-2000||10:45 AM||||You can verify the correct ECM and PROM for your car using the data at this link:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cruzers.com/~ludis/fieroprom.html#L4" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cruzers.com/~ludis/fieroprom.html#L4</A> <P><BR>It is possible that a previous owner could have done an engine swap, but if it really is the "wrong" ECM for your motor, I would highly suggest a replacement.<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||03-09-2000||11:02 AM||||Just how did you figure it was the wrong ECM/PROM????<P>The tranny doesn't matter as far as the ECM itself. The PROM does matter wether it's stick or auto. It will likely run, but you're also likely to get wierd errors if you mismatch tranny and PROM.<P>here's a brief summary of what's diferant. It's not everything thats diferant, but most of it. (((DIS motor also added an air charge temp sensor in the manifold right of the TBI)))<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||03-09-2000||11:47 AM||||If it's a 4 cylinder, it's a 6156 or 7148. The 7148 is the DIS ECM. The 6156 is the HEI.<P>If it's a 6 cylinder, it's a 6869 in 85 and 7170 in 86-88.<P>From memory, but I think those are right.<P>The 6156 and 7148 were used in many different cars. <P>Ludis' site is a great place to nail it down.||||reg||1
